# The annotations of the menu image: the single source of truth for src/main/*.asm (kit/mkgame.py).
# Format: ../../../../docs/ann-format.md.
#
# ZYX boot (ZYX Software, 1993-1994): the two pieces the BASIC loader (src/basic, Loader $5D80) reads after the
# program: 15 sectors of code and data at $61A8-$70A7 and 8 sectors of graphics at $A600-$ADFF. The image runs
# $61A8-$ADFF; $70A8-$A5FF is never loaded: the loader clears it, and the set-up builds the rhombus phases and keeps
# the catalogue there (the buffers module). The music, 22 sectors at $C000, is the part src/music.
# Witness: "L1 N (builds)" is the runs of an entry's first instruction summed over c1-c4 (case.json code_builds);
# kit/mkgame.py --fix-witness writes the real counts. "Known only from the code" marks what no recording ran.
#
# Credit: Vadim's decompilation (work/candidates/sources-2010-vadim/ZYXboot.mac, "Decompiled by Vadim (C) July
# 2010") was the starting point for the names, the routine boundaries and the data shapes; assembled with sjasmplus it
# gives this image's bytes $61A8-$705F exactly (work/stage2/vadim.asm). Every name here was checked against the code
# and the recordings; work/stage2/NOTES.md lists each of Vadim's names and the name it has here, and why.

@equ $1601 RomChanOpen ; ROM: open the channel A (2: the upper screen)
@equ $3D13 TrdosEntry ; the TR-DOS ROM's entry for machine code: C = 5 reads B sectors from track D, sector E to HL
@equ $4000 Screen 6144 ; the screen's pixels
@equ $5800 Attributes 768 ; the screen's attributes
@equ $5C36 Chars 2 ; ROM variable CHARS: the font's address - 256
@equ $5C78 Frames 2 ; ROM variable FRAMES: the menu's interrupt counter (Interrupt $6757 adds 1, the menu resets it)
@equ $5D4C LaunchToken 1 ; in the BASIC: line 1's command token (src/basic: LaunchLine)
@equ $AFC8 SavedIY 2 ; IY at the entry (the ROM's $5C3A), put back for every ROM call
@equ $AFCA SavedHLAlt 2 ; HL' at the entry, which BASIC needs unchanged when the USR returns, put back for every ROM call
@equ $AFCC SavedSP 2 ; SP at the entry: the launcher returns to the loader with it
@equ $AFCE SavedI 1 ; I at the entry
@equ $B3B0 MainTick 1 ; the main loop's pass counter
@equ $B3B1 PhaseTopLeft 1 ; the step 0-31 in PhaseOrder of the top left rhombus
@equ $B3B2 PhaseTopRight 1 ; the step of the top right rhombus
@equ $B3B3 PhaseBottomLeft 1 ; the step of the bottom left rhombus
@equ $B3B4 PhaseBottomRight 1 ; the step of the bottom right rhombus
@equ $B3B5 FileCount 1 ; the files the page lists (from the page's first entry to the catalogue's end)
@equ $B3B6 CursorRow 1 ; the cursor's screen row (6-17; 11 for NO EXE FILES !)
@equ $B3B7 CursorColumn 1 ; the cursor's screen column: 14 (one column), 6 or 22 (two columns)
@equ $B3B8 Columns 1 ; the page's columns: 0 (no files), 1 or 2
@equ $B3B9 MorePages 1 ; 1: SYMBOL SHIFT shows the next page (more than 24 files from this page on)
@equ $B3BA ColumnsSpare 2 ; cleared with Columns, not read
@equ $B3BB ColumnOneCount 1 ; the files in column 1
@equ $B3BC ColumnTwoCount 1 ; the files in column 2 (0: one column)
@equ $B3BE CursorLength 1 ; the cursor's width in cells: 8 (a name) or 14 (NO EXE FILES !)
@equ $B3BF CursorAttr 1 ; the cursor's attribute this time (Interrupt cycles it)
@equ $B3C0 CursorIndex 1 ; the cursor's file, 1-24 down column 1 then column 2
@equ $B3C1 PagePointer 2 ; the catalogue entry the page starts at (Catalogue $9D00 on the first page)
@equ $B3C3 NextPagePointer 2 ; the entry after the page's 24th file
@equ $B3C9 CursorAttrOffset 2 ; CursorAttrAddr's last result - $5800
@equ $B3CB CursorSavedAttr 1 ; the attribute under the cursor before it was drawn (the file's ink)
@equ $B3CC RotateRow 2 ; the row and column of the name being rotated
@equ $B3CE EntryLine 22 ; one catalogue line: AT row,col BRIGHT 1 INK 5 "X>  " INK n, the name, $FF
@equ $B3EC RotateLength 1 ; the rotated name's width in cells
@equ $B3ED CharBuffer 8 ; one character's 8 rows while it is rotated
@equ $DEA8 RomPointer 2 ; NewRandoms's index into the ROM's first 2K
@equ $DEAA TwinkleAddr 2 ; the twinkle's screen address
@equ $DEAC TwinkleClock 1 ; +2 a frame: the twinkle's phase and place
@equ $DEAE RotateAddr 2 ; the rotated name's address in ScreenCopy
@equ $DEB0 TwinklePause 1 ; counts the 8 frames between two twinkles
@equ $E000 ScreenCopy 6912 ; the clean screen: pixels $E000, attributes $F800; the twinkles, the glint and the rotated name restore from it
@equ $FCBC MenuStack 1 ; the menu's stack top
@equ $FCFC Im2Jump 3 ; JP Interrupt, where every IM 2 vector points
@equ $FD00 Im2Table 257 ; the IM 2 vector table: 257 bytes of $FC

@const MusicInit $C00B ; the music piece's init entry (src/music: $C00B); a variant's music piece has its own (variants/)
@const ScreenCopyHigh $E0 ; OR'ed into a screen address's high byte: the same place in ScreenCopy ($80 where a variant keeps the copy at $C000)
@const GlintEdge $02 ; the glint's outer cells: red ink (GlintRed)
@const GlintCentre $42 ; the glint's middle cell: bright red ink (GlintBright)
@const MusicPlay $C00E ; the music piece's one-frame entry, called by Interrupt
@isub $61E7 CALL MusicInit
@isub $61D2 LD (HL),Im2Jump>>8
@isub $61E3 LD A,Im2Table>>8
@isub $67D2 OR ScreenCopy>>8
@isub $6C86 OR ScreenCopyHigh
@isub $6D71 OR ScreenCopyHigh
@isub $6CA6 AND $FF^(ScreenCopy>>8^$40)
@isub $676D CALL MusicPlay
@isub $6DB7 LD (HL),GlintEdge
@isub $6DC3 LD (HL),GlintCentre
@isub $6E41 LD HL,30000
@isub $6508 LD BC,-2048+32

@module setup ; The entry: save the caller's state, read the catalogue, IM 2, draw the screen, build the phases, the main loop
@about Start $61A8 is the menu's only entry, called by the BASIC loader (src/basic, Loader $5D80) once the three pieces are in memory. It runs once from the CALL to the first file chosen: the launcher RunLetter $6A51 puts the saved SP back and returns to the loader.
@about MainLoop $627C never waits for the interrupt: it runs as fast as the CPU allows between the interrupts, so the rhombi turn and the stars fall at a speed that depends on the machine.

$61A8 c Start ; Save the caller's registers, read the catalogue, set up IM 2 and the music, draw the screen and build the rhombus phases
$6220 StartMirrorA
$6248 StartMirrorB
$6256 StartTurnB
> Saves IY (SavedIY $AFC8), HL' (SavedHLAlt), SP (SavedSP) and I (SavedI). Reads 8 sectors from track 0, sector 0 (the catalogue) to Catalogue $9D00 through TrdosEntry $3D13, still in IM 1. Then SP = MenuStack $FCBC, DI, Im2Table $FD00 filled with $FC, JP Interrupt at Im2Jump $FCFC, IM 2 with I = $FD. CALL $C00B (the music's init, src/music), DrawScreen $6510 (which also lists the catalogue), EI.
> The rhombi: copies RombSource $6E98 to RombWork $7700, RombColumnOrder to RombColumns $7600 and RombPhaseOffsets to RombOffsets $765C; BuildRombPhases $62F1 with threshold $40 writes set A, phases 0-8 at PhasesA $7800-$80FF, and MirrorSprite $63D6 the mirror images of phases 0-7 as phases 9-16 at MirrorsA $8100-$88FF. RotateSprite $6422 three times turns RombWork by 270 degrees; BuildRombPhases (threshold $80) writes set B at PhasesB $8A00, MirrorSprite the mirrors at MirrorsB $9300, and RotateSprite turns each of the 17 sprites $8A00-$9AFF by 90 degrees: set A turns about the vertical axis, set B about the horizontal one.
> DrawReflection $6DC6 draws the logo's mirror image at the bottom; the four rhombus steps start at 0, 5, 8 and 14 (PhaseTopLeft $B3B1-PhaseBottomRight $B3B4), MainTick at $FF; SampleStars $645E reads the 60 stars' pixels; then falls into MainLoop.
> Witness: L1 4 (c x4).

$627C c MainLoop ; The main loop: the falling stars and the turning rhombi, as fast as the CPU runs
> MainTick $B3B0 + 1 each pass. Every 2nd pass: MoveStars $6482 twice on StarsA $AC84 and twice on StarsB $ACFC (the operand MoveStars+2 set first). Every 4th: the top left rhombus (set A, at $4000, PhaseTopLeft) and the top right one (set B, at $401B, PhaseTopRight) one step (PrintRombPhase $64DA), and StarsC $AD74 once. Every 8th: the bottom left rhombus (set B, at $5060) and the bottom right (set A, at $507B). No HALT: the loop never ends; Interrupt $6757 does the rest (keys, music, twinkles, cursor) and the launcher leaves through the stack.
> Witness: L1 27670 (c x4).
$62A4 MainEvery4 ; every 4th pass: the top rhombi, StarsC
$62CE MainEvery8 ; every 8th pass: the bottom rhombi

@module rhombi ; The four corner rhombi: their 34 phases built at the start, printed one step at a time
@about The rhombus (RombSource $6E98, src/res/graphics/rhombus.png) is a 40x40 sprite: 5 bytes a row, 40 rows. Its turn is faked by dropping columns: phase k (0-7) keeps 40 - 5k of the 40 pixel columns, dropped in the order RombColumnOrder $6B74 gives, packed together and placed RombPhaseOffsets[8 - k] pixels from the left so the narrowed picture stays centred. Phase 8 is the rhombus edge-on, phases 9-16 the mirror images of phases 0-7 (the back face). PhaseOrder $6ADD walks 32 steps: 0-7, 8, 16-9, 10-16, 8, 7-1: a full turn.
@about Phase 8 is drawn only where a column's order value is at or above the threshold ($40 for set A, $80 for set B); the values are 0-39, so phase 8 stays the blank sprite the loader's clear left: the rhombus vanishes edge-on (known only from the code).

$62F1 c BuildRombPhases ; Build phases 0-7 and the edge-on phase 8 of the rhombus in RombWork, at DE (256 bytes a phase)
$62F7 BuildPhase
$62FC BuildPhaseRow
> In: DE = the set's base (PhasesA $7800 or PhasesB $8A00), RombThreshold $768E. For each phase (C = 8 down to 1) and each of the 40 rows: ClearPhaseMask $633B sets PhaseOffset $76C0 = RombOffsets[C] and clears PhaseMask $7698; the first 5 x C entries of RombColumns mark their columns in PhaseMask (MarkPhaseColumn $6354); ScaleRombLine $6364 writes the row. Each phase starts at the next 256 bytes (D + 1, E = 0).
> Then phase 8 at DE: for each row k, $18 at byte 2 when RombColumns[k] >= the threshold (never: see the module's header).
> Witness: L1 8 (c x4).
$6304 MarkColumns ; mark the first 5 x C columns
$6306 MarkColumn
$6329 EdgeOnRow ; phase 8: the edge-on line
$6331 EdgeOnNext

$633B c ClearPhaseMask ; Clear PhaseMask and set PhaseOffset to RombOffsets[C]
$634D ClearPhaseIndex
> The offset's index is written into the LD A,(IY+d) at $634D (IY = RombOffsets $765C).
> Witness: L1 2560 (c x4).

$6354 c MarkPhaseColumn ; PhaseMask[A] = 1: column A is kept in this phase
$635D MarkPhaseWrite
> The index is written into the LD (IX+d),1 at $635D.
> Witness: L1 57600 (c x4).

$6364 c ScaleRombLine ; Copy one 40-pixel row of the rhombus to the phase: only the marked columns, packed, from PhaseOffset on
> In: HL = the source row, DE = the phase row, PhaseMask $7698, PhaseOffset $76C0 (bits 7-3 the byte, 2-0 the pixel). For each of the 40 source pixels whose PhaseMask byte is 1, writes the pixel (SET or RES, the opcode built into the instruction at $63AD) at the next destination pixel. Returns HL and DE 5 bytes on (the next row).
> Witness: L1 2560 (c x4).
$638B ScaleNextByte ; the next source byte
$638D ScaleNextBit ; the next source bit: the BIT n,(IX) at $6392 built from E
$6392 ScaleTestBit
$639A ScaleHaveBit
$63AD ScaleWriteBit ; RES / SET n,(IY+0): the opcode written above
$63BB ScaleSkipBit

$63D6 c MirrorSprite ; Write the mirror image (left to right) of the 40x40 sprite at HL to DE
$63DE MirrorRow
> Reverses the 5 bytes of each row and the bits of each byte (MirrorByte $6419). HL = DE is allowed (FlipSprite $6E35 uses it so).
> Witness: L1 80 (c x4).

$6419 c MirrorByte ; Reverse the bits of C
$641B MirrorByteBit
> Witness: L1 16000 (c x4).

$6422 c RotateSprite ; Turn the 40x40 sprite at HL by 90 degrees (through RotateBuffer $9C00)
$6428 RotateColumnByte
$642C RotateColumnBit
$6430 RotateSourceRow
$6436 RotateShiftRow
> For each of the 40 source columns (left to right), shifts the column's 40 pixels into one 40-pixel row of RotateBuffer $9C00, then copies the 200 bytes back to HL. Called 3 times on RombWork, 17 times on set B, twice by FlipSprite for each logo sprite.
> Witness: L1 112 (c x4).

@module stars ; The falling stars and the twinkles
@about 60 stars fall down the screen, one pixel row at a time: three tables of 20 (StarsA $AC84 and StarsB $ACFC moved twice every 2nd pass of MainLoop, StarsC $AD74 once every 4th pass: the third group falls 4 times slower), each star one pixel, the tables' stars in pairs side by side (src/res/tables/stars.txt). A star is drawn by setting its pixel; before it moves, the screen pixel under it is put back (SET if it was lit, RES if not), so the stars pass over the menu without damaging it. From the bottom row a star goes back to the top.
@about The twinkle is one 16x16 star sprite (TwinkleSprites $6F60, src/res/graphics/twinkle.png) at a random place: it grows through 4 frames and shrinks back, one frame every 4 interrupts, lit 30 frames and dark 9: a new place every 39 interrupts (TwinkleStar $6BA5).

$645E c SampleStars ; Read the screen pixel under each of the 60 stars into its record
> Falls into SampleStar with IX = StarsA $AC84, B = 60 (the three tables follow each other).
> Witness: L1 12 (c x4).

$6464 c SampleStar ; For B stars from IX: record +5 = the pixel under the star (BIT n,(HL), the opcode at +4)
> A star's record (6 bytes): +0/+1 the screen address, +2 RES n,(HL)'s second byte, +3 SET n,(HL)'s, +4 BIT n,(HL)'s, +5 the pixel under it (0 or 1).
> Witness: L1 1245596 (c x4).
$6471 SampleStarBit ; BIT n,(HL): the opcode from the record
$6477 SampleStarStore

$6482 c MoveStars ; Move the 20 stars of one table down one pixel row
$6488 MoveStarsNext
$649E MoveStarsRestore
> The table's address is the operand of the LD IX at $6482 (MainLoop writes it). For each star: puts back the screen pixel under it (RES or SET from +2/+3 by +5), moves the address one pixel row down (from the bottom of the screen to the top), samples the new pixel (SampleStar with B = 1) and sets it.
> Witness: L1 62246 (c x4).
$64A1 MoveStarsPutBack ; RES / SET n,(HL): put back the pixel the star covered
$64C1 MoveStarsMoved
$64D4 MoveStarsDraw ; SET n,(HL): draw the star at its new place

@module rhombi
$64DA c PrintRombPhase ; Print the rhombus at its step (BC) at HL from the phase set at DE, and advance the step
> The phase is PhaseOrder[(BC)], its sprite DE + 256 x phase; PrintSprite $64F4. The step (BC) + 1, modulo 32.
> Witness: L1 20744 (c x4).

$64F4 c PrintSprite ; Print a 40x40 sprite from DE to the screen at HL (5 cells across, 5 down)
$64F6 PrintSpriteCellRow
$64F9 PrintSpriteLine
> Plain copy (no masking). Used for the rhombi, the logo and, into ScreenCopy, the reflection.
> Witness: L1 20792 (c x4).

@module screen ; The menu's screen, the pages and the cursor
@about DrawScreen $6510 draws the static screen once: the four rhombi (phase 0), the ZYX logo (Logo $A900 at columns 6-25, rows 0-4, with LogoAttributes $AC20), the corner gradients (ColourCorners $6CBF), the help lines (MenuHelp $6AFD, rows 19-21) and saves it to ScreenCopy $E000. ShowCatalogue $6586 then puts that screen back, clears rows 6-17 and lists a page: up to 12 files in one column (names at column 14), 13-24 in two (columns 6 and 22), 24 a page when there are more (SYMBOL SHIFT for the next page, CAPS SHIFT for the first). Each line is "X>  name": the letter that runs it, in bright cyan, the name green (a BASIC program) or yellow (a 192-sector CODE file).
@about The cursor is the attribute of its name's 8 cells: every 4th interrupt Interrupt $6757 writes CursorAttr $B3BF there and steps it through inks 1-7 (alternately plain and bright); when it moves, the old cells get back CursorSavedAttr $B3CB.

$6510 c DrawScreen ; Draw the static screen, save it to ScreenCopy and fall into ShowCatalogue
$655D DrawLogoAttrRow
> The rhombi at $4000, $401B, $5060, $507B (RombSource), the logo's four sprites at $4006, $400B, $4010, $4015, its attributes (5 rows of 20 from LogoAttributes $AC20 to $5806), ColourCorners $6CBF, Chars $5C36 = Font - 256, MenuHelp through PrintString $6720; copies the screen to ScreenCopy $E000.
> Witness: L1 4 (c x4).

$6586 c ShowCatalogue ; Show a catalogue page: the clean screen, the file list, the cursor; save the screen
> SoundOff $6E41; ScreenCopy back to the screen; ClearFilesArea $6930; CursorAttr = 1; ListCatalogue $65C7 from the entry at the operand of the LD IX at $659C (ShowCatalogueAt: Catalogue $9D00, or the next page's for SYMBOL SHIFT); CursorSavedAttr = the attribute under the cursor; PagePointer $B3C1 = the page's first entry; Frames $5C78 and TwinkleClock $DEAC = 0; the screen to ScreenCopy again (now with the list); SampleStars $645E.
> Called from DrawScreen (falling in), ReadKeyboard for CAPS SHIFT, SYMBOL SHIFT and SPACE.
> Witness: L1 8 (c x4).
$659C ShowCatalogueAt ; LD IX,nn: the page's first catalogue entry

$65C7 c ListCatalogue ; Count the page's files from IX and print them in one or two columns, or NO EXE FILES !
$65D0 CountNext
$65E1 CountCode
$65EC ; exactly 192 sectors ($C0): a 48K Magic snapshot. Vadim's comment reads 'length >= C000'; the test is CP $C0 with JR NZ, equality only
$65F1 CountSkip
$65F9 CountDone
> Counts the entries from IX to the catalogue's end (a first byte 0, or 128 entries: the end of Catalogue at $A500) that the menu lists: type B except "boot    B" (IsBootFile $6737), and type C of exactly 192 sectors ($C0 at +13; a 48K snapshot saved as CODE, which TR-DOS runs with GO TO "name" CODE). Deleted entries (first byte 1) are skipped (SkipDeleted $674D).
> 0 files: NoFilesText at row 11, column 9, the cursor 14 cells wide on it. 1-12: one column (PrintColumn $668E at row 6, column 10, Columns = 1). 13-24: ListTwoColumns $6648. 25 or more: ListTwoColumns with 24, NextPagePointer $B3C3 = the entry after the 24th, MorePages $B3B9 = 1.
> Witness: L1 8 (c x4).
$661E ListOneColumn ; 1-12 files: one column
$6644 ListTwoOrMore

$6648 c ListTwoColumns ; Print A files (13-24) in two columns: half (rounded up) in column 1 at column 2, the rest at column 18
> ColumnOneCount $B3BB = A - A / 2, ColumnTwoCount $B3BC = A / 2; the cursor on the first file (row 6, column 6).
> Witness: L1 3 (c x2).
$6680 ListPaged ; 25 or more: 24 now, the rest on the next page

$668E c PrintColumn ; Print D catalogue lines from HL, the first at row C, column B, the letters from E
$6692 PrintColumnNext
$6693 PrintColumnEntry
$66AF ; exactly 192 sectors ($C0): a 48K Magic snapshot. Vadim's comment reads 'length >= C000'; the test is CP $C0 with JR Z, equality only
$66B3 PrintColumnSkip
> Each listed entry: EntryLine $B3CE = AT row,col, BRIGHT 1, INK 5, letter, ">", two spaces, INK 4 (BASIC) or INK 6 (192-sector CODE), the 8 name characters (a byte below 32 or from 128 up shown as "."), $FF; PrintString $6720. Returns HL after the last entry printed.
> Witness: L1 8 (c x2).
$66B8 PrintColumnBasic ; a BASIC file: green
$66BE PrintColumnName ; the name: 8 characters, the unprintable ones as "."
$66CD PrintNameChar
$66D4 PrintNameHigh
$66DA PrintNameStore

@module font ; Printing: the ROM's RST $10 with the menu's font
@about The menu prints through the ROM (RST $10 on channel 2) with Chars $5C36 pointing at Font $A600 - 256 (src/res/graphics/font.png: 96 characters, space to the copyright sign). The texts are MenuHelp $6AFD and NoFilesText $6B41 (src/res/texts) and the catalogue lines built in EntryLine $B3CE. The launcher sets Chars back to the ROM's $3C00.

$6720 c PrintString ; Print the string at HL up to $FF through RST $10 on channel 2
$672F PrintStringNext
> Puts back HL' and IY for the ROM (SavedHLAlt, SavedIY), opens channel 2 (RomChanOpen $1601), then RST $10 for each byte: the control codes AT, INK, BRIGHT are the ROM's.
> Witness: L1 93 (c x4).

@module catalogue ; Reading the TR-DOS catalogue
@about The catalogue is track 0's sectors 0-7 (128 entries of 16 bytes: name 8, type 1, start 2, length 2, sectors 1, first sector 1, track 1) read to Catalogue $9D00 by Start and again by SPACE. A first byte 0 ends it, 1 marks a deleted file.

$6737 c IsBootFile ; Compare the 9 bytes at HL (name and type) with "boot    B": C = 0 if equal, $FF if not
$673F IsBootNext
$6745 IsBootSame
> Returns HL at the entry's type byte (+8) and E = C: the caller ANDs the type with E, so the boot file's type reads 0 and is never listed.
> Witness: L1 219 (c x4).

$674D c SkipDeleted ; While the entry at HL is deleted (A = 1), go to the next one (HL + 16, A its first byte)
> Witness: L1 234 (c x4).

@module interrupt ; The IM 2 interrupt: the music, the clocks, the twinkle, the glint, the cursor, the name's spin, the keys
@about Interrupt $6757 runs at every frame through Im2Jump $FCFC. It saves every register, puts back HL' and IY for the ROM, plays the music (CALL $C00E, src/music), adds 1 to Frames $5C78 and 2 to TwinkleClock $DEAC, draws the twinkle (TwinkleStar $6BA5) and the logo's glint (LogoGlint $6D04). Every 4th frame it paints the cursor. When Frames' low byte is 50, 60, 70 or 80 (in every 256 frames, 5.12 s) the cursor's name is turned by 90 degrees, so it spins once; at 90 the screen gets the name as it stands. Every 8th frame ReadKeyboard $6864 reads the keys.

$6757 c Interrupt ; The IM 2 handler
$6796 PaintCursor
$67AB StoreCursorAttr
> Witness: L1 13932 (c x4).
$67AF CheckSpin ; Frames low byte 50: spin the cursor's name

$67C2 c RotateCursorName ; Turn each character of the cursor's name by 90 degrees in ScreenCopy
> RotateAddr $DEAE = the name's address in ScreenCopy (from CursorAttrAddr's offset); each character's 8 rows to CharBuffer $B3ED, then written back column by column. At Frames 90 the LD A at $67D8 is made a RET for one call: only the address is worked out.
> Witness: L1 276 (c x4).
$67D8 SpinCharacters ; LD A,(RotateLength): a RET at Frames 90
$67DC RotateNextChar ; the next character
$67E4 RotateCopyRow
$67EF RotateColumn
$67F2 RotateColumnShift

$6803 c ShowRotatedName ; Copy the name from ScreenCopy to the screen (CopyNameToScreen) and leave
$680E CheckSpinMore
> Witness: L1 276 (c x4).

$682D c InterruptExit ; Every 8th frame read the keys; restore the registers, EI, RET
> Witness: L1 13931 (c x4).

@module screen
$6848 c CursorAttrAddr ; HL = the attribute address of the row and column at the operand of the LD DE at $6848 (CursorRow normally)
$6852 AttrRowLoop
$685A AttrAddColumn
> Also CursorAttrOffset $B3C9 = HL - $5800. RotateCursorName points the operand at RotateRow for one call.
> Witness: L1 3784 (c x4).

@module keys ; The keys, every 8th frame
@about ReadKeyboard $6864: the letters A-X (KeyTable $6B53, src/res/tables/keys.txt) run the file with that letter at once (RunLetter $6A51); 1, 2, 6, 7 change the column, 3 and 8 move down, 4 and 9 up, 5, 0 and ENTER run the file under the cursor (the two Sinclair joysticks: 1-5 and 6-0 are left, right, down, up, fire); CAPS SHIFT shows the first page, SYMBOL SHIFT the next one, SPACE reads the catalogue again (a new disk). A Kempston read is replaced by LD A,0: its five CALL C never call. The keys repeat while held: at 8 frames a read, a held key moves the cursor 6 times a second.

$6864 c ReadKeyboard ; Read the letters, the digits, ENTER, CAPS SHIFT, SYMBOL SHIFT and SPACE
> Witness: L1 1741 (c x4).
$6869 ReadNextLetter ; the next letter: the half-row from bits 7-5, the key from bits 4-0
$6875 ReadLetterRow ; RES n,A: the half-row's address bit, the opcode from the table
$68B9 Joystick ; the Kempston read: LD A,$00, so the five CALL C after it never call (variants with IN A,($1F) read the joystick, variants/README.md)
$68E5 ReadEnter ; ENTER: fire
$6915 ReadSymbolShift ; SYMBOL SHIFT: the next page, when there is one

@module screen
$6930 c ClearFilesArea ; Clear the pixels of rows 6-17 and set their attributes to white on black
$6935 ClearRow
$6937 ClearCell
$6959 ClearAttrs
> Witness: L1 8 (c x4).

$6967 c ColumnKey ; 1, 2, 6, 7: move the cursor to the other column (two columns only)
> From column 1 to column 2 the cursor keeps its row, but from column 1's last file, when column 2 is one shorter (13-23 files odd), it goes to column 1's first (CursorIndex 1, row 6, the column unchanged); from column 2 to column 1 it keeps the row.
> Witness: L1 2 (c x1).

$696E c KeyDone ; Pop the caller's AF and return: the key had nothing to do
$6970 SwapColumn
$697A SwapRestoreAttr
$69A1 ToColumnTwo
$69AD ToColumnOne
> Witness: L1 1 (c x1).

$69B7 c CursorMoved ; CursorSavedAttr = the attribute at the cursor's new place; pop AF, return
> Witness: L1 8 (c x1).

$69C0 c DownKey ; 3, 8: the cursor down one file (B = 0), wrapping from the column's last file to its first
> Witness: L1 4 (c x1).

$69C3 c MoveCursor ; Move the cursor down (B = 0) or up (B = 1), wrapping within its column; nothing with a single file
$69D2 MoveCursorGo
$69DD MoveRestoreAttr
$6A01 MoveDownOne
$6A07 MoveUpColumnOne
$6A10 MoveUpOne
$6A16 MoveColumnTwo
$6A25 MoveUpColumnTwo
